Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I know lavaan and other softwares does not include the option to fit a whole structural equation model SEM with outcome variables that are not continuous (there is only a workaround for binary variables).

That is not true.  The same method for binary variables is applied to ordinal variables.  It is statistically equivalent to a cumulative probit model (or just probit for binary outcomes), but the logit link is not currently available.  Just declare the binary/ordinal variables as ordered by listing them in the ordered= argument.  The default estimator will be DWLS (WLSMV) but ULSMV and PML are available alternatives.  They accept any mixture of continuous and ordered outcomes.

> I was wondering if it is correct to calculate the factor scores using the lavPredict() function

Yes, you can get factor scores even when indicators are ordered, but SEs are not available to quantify their uncertainty.  Thus, it is not yet possible to draw several plausible values of factor scores in order to properly account for their uncertainty in a subsequent analyses.  See examples and references on the ?semTools::plausibleValues help page.

Terrence D. Jorgensen
Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
