From u1ff876476824ff7e at groups.google.com  Wed Jun  9 04:56:43 2021
From: u1ff876476824ff7e at groups.google.com (=?utf-8?q?Rebekka_Lagac=C3=A9-Cusiac?=)
Date: Wed, 09 Jun 2021 04:56:43 +0000
Subject: Fitting bifactor models while controlling for other latent variables
Message-ID: <NogoSmLhJJI.0hYdxROeBwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/NogoSmLhJJI/m/0hYdxROeBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,
I'm interested in measuring how much variance across six tasks is explained by a general factor vs two specific factors. To do this, I wanted to fit a bifactor model while controlling for other latent variables.
This is the initial model I wanted to estimate:

This is how I programmed it in lavaan:
modelB.21 <- "

SFA=~ T1+ T2+ T3
SFB=~ T4+ T5+ T6
GF=~  T1  +  T2  +  T3  +  T4  +  T5  + T6

CFA=~ CT1+  CT2 + CT3
CFB=~  CT4 +  CT5 + CT6

T1~ CFA
T2~ CFA
T3~ CFA
T4~ CFB
T5~ CFB
T6~ CFB

GF~~ 0*SFA
GF~~ 0*SFB
SFA~~ 0*SFB

GF~~ 0*CFA
GF~~ 0*CFB
SFA~~ 0*CFA
SFA~~ 0*CFB
SFB~~ 0*CFA
SFB~~ 0*CFB

"
This model did not converge. However, when I implement it this way, it does:

modelB.4 <- "

CFA=~ CT1+  CT2 + CT3
CFB=~  CT4 +  CT5 + CT6

T1~ CFA
T2~ CFA
T3~ CFA
T4~ CFB
T5~ CFB
T6~ CFB

res_T1=~ T1
res_T2=~ T2
res_T3=~ T3
res_T4=~ T4
res_T5=~ T5
res_T6=~ T6

SFA=~ res_T1+ res_T2+ res_T3
SFB=~ res_T4+ res_T5+ res_T6
GF=~ res_T1  +  res_T2  +  res_T3  +  res_T4  +  res_T5  + res_T6

GF~~ 0*SFA
GF~~ 0*SFB
SFA~~ 0*SFB

GF~~ 0*CFA
GF~~ 0*CFB
SFA~~ 0*CFA
SFA~~ 0*CFB
SFB~~ 0*CFA
SFB~~ 0*CFB

res_T1 ~~ 0*CFA
res_T2 ~~ 0*CFA
res_T3 ~~ 0*CFA
res_T4 ~~ 0*CFA
res_T5 ~~ 0*CFA
res_T6 ~~ 0*CFA

res_T1 ~~ 0*CFB
res_T2 ~~ 0*CFB
res_T3 ~~ 0*CFB
res_T4 ~~ 0*CFB
res_T5 ~~ 0*CFB
res_T6 ~~ 0*CFB
"
This model converges when I remove 5 influential outliers on a sample size 271 participants. However, it warns me about a negative/near zero eigen value only when I fit the model to the entire data . Some observed variables deviate significantly from normality, so I'm using robust methods. Given that both outputs(with and without influential outliers given me similar estimates, I wonder if the second model is empirically underidentified when we keep those influential outliers (which would explain why it didn't converge when I kept the influential outliers).

On a side note, I also tried fitting just the bifactor model. I thought this model was unidentified, but it still seems to converge. Does this mean I cannot trust the parameter estimates even if it gives me a solution?

To summarize, my main questions are: which of these models are identified and can I trust a model if it looks like it converge properly?

Any help would be appreciated :)

