Dear Terrence,

Query 1: If there is no missing data, but data is non=normal, and we are using robust estimators like MLM in sem() to contain non-normalcy of data; then should we prefer montecarlo method to bootstrap method (since bootstrap does not run with MLR or MLM estimators)?
Query 2:
I read following comment by you  "If both the indirect and direct effects are significant, that is consistent with partial mediation.  If only the indirect is significant, that is consistent with full mediation.  If only the direct is significant, that is consistent with no mediation." on following thread: https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/1twNwxvrg7o/m/BkeAxhR8CAAJ

How to interpret full, partial or no mediation when we are using monte carlo method?
Do we have to run following codes (for the sake of examples)?:
mont_indirect <- monteCarloMed('a*b', object = fit, plot=T, CI=95)
mont_indirect
mont_direct <- monteCarloMed('c', object = fit, plot=T, CI=95)
mont_direct
mont_total <- monteCarloMed('a*b+c', object = fit, plot=T, CI=95)
mont_total

And then we compare significance of mont_indirect and mont_direct to draw conclusions regarding full, partial, or no mediation. If yes, then what is the role of calculating mont_total, i.e. total effect?

On Wednesday, March 31, 2021 at 2:27:35 AM UTC+5:30 Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
> > would the Monte Carlo perform as well as bootstrapping in a large sample (e.g. N>10.000) ?
>
> I'm sure, but it depends on the model size.  In simple mediation models, the methods (including delta method) practically converge in samples barely over 100.
>
> Terrence D. Jorgensen
> Assistant Professor, Methods and Statistics
> Research Institute for Child Development and Education, the University of Amsterdam
> http://www.uva.nl/profile/t.d.jorgensen
>

