> > Hi, Harmanjit.
> >
> > Q1. It's a good question, but the answer is that it doesn't matter. The adjustments in MLR are to the standard errors, not the point estimates. The bootstrapped confidence intervals do not rely on the standard errors.
> >
> > Q2. The total effect is a derived quantity (direct + indirect) that is often of substantive interest (X  on Y regardless of path to get there), but is not in itself part of the estimated model.
> >
> > Pat
> >
> > On Fri, Jun 11, 2021 at 4:32 AM Harmanjit Singh <harman...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > Dear Terrence,
> > >
> > > Query 1: If there is no missing data, but data is non=normal, and we are using robust estimators like MLM in sem() to contain non-normalcy of data; then should we prefer montecarlo method to bootstrap method (since bootstrap does not run with MLR or MLM estimators)?
> > > Query 2:
> > > I read following comment by you  "If both the indirect and direct effects are significant, that is consistent with partial mediation.  If only the indirect is significant, that is consistent with full mediation.  If only the direct is significant, that is consistent with no mediation." on following thread: https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/1twNwxvrg7o/m/BkeAxhR8CAAJ
