On Friday, June 11, 2021 at 11:05:20 PM UTC+5:30 Pat Malone wrote:
> No.  I'm saying that using a robust estimator does not affect the decision whether to use MCMC or bootstrapping.
>
>
>
> Patrick S. Malone, PhD
> Sr Research Statistician
> FAR HARBΦR
> +1 803.553.4181 | pat@ | farharbor.com <http://farharbor.com>
> This message may contain confidential information; if you are not the intended recipient please notify the sender and delete the message.
> On Fri, Jun 11, 2021, 1:32 PM Harmanjit Singh <harman...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > What I interpret from your current response is that MCMC is a better approach when using robust estimator, compared to bootstrapping method.
> > Kindly acknowledge whether I have understood correctly or not?
> >
> > On Friday, June 11, 2021 at 6:40:15 PM UTC+5:30 Pat Malone wrote:
> > > I misunderstood. The MCMC method does use SEs, so it does matter. But you can get the robust SEs for MCMC if you're not bootstrapping, and bootstrapping doesn't use SEs, so I'm no longer sure what the conflict is.
> > >
> > > On Fri, Jun 11, 2021 at 8:25 AM Harmanjit Singh <harman...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > Thanks Patrick.
> > > >
> > > > Q1. I asked this, if I prefer to use MonteCarlo method in the above scenario and reviewer questions why you didn't use bootstrapped, what could be a better rationale to defend this choice?
> > > > Q2. Thanks
> > > >
> > > > On Friday, June 11, 2021 at 5:36:24 PM UTC+5:30 Pat Malone wrote:
> > > > > Hi, Harmanjit.
> > > > >
> > > > > Q1. It's a good question, but the answer is that it doesn't matter. The adjustments in MLR are to the standard errors, not the point estimates. The bootstrapped confidence intervals do not rely on the standard errors.
> > > > >
> > > > > Q2. The total effect is a derived quantity (direct + indirect) that is often of substantive interest (X  on Y regardless of path to get there), but is not in itself part of the estimated model.
> > > > >
> > > > > Pat
> > > > >
> > > > > On Fri, Jun 11, 2021 at 4:32 AM Harmanjit Singh <harman...@gmail.com> wrote:
> > > > > > Dear Terrence,
> > > > > >
> > > > > > Query 1: If there is no missing data, but data is non=normal, and we are using robust estimators like MLM in sem() to contain non-normalcy of data; then should we prefer montecarlo method to bootstrap method (since bootstrap does not run with MLR or MLM estimators)?
> > > > > > Query 2:
> > > > > > I read following comment by you  "If both the indirect and direct effects are significant, that is consistent with partial mediation.  If only the indirect is significant, that is consistent with full mediation.  If only the direct is significant, that is consistent with no mediation." on following thread: https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/1twNwxvrg7o/m/BkeAxhR8CAAJ
> > > > > >
> > > > > > How to interpret full, partial or no mediation when we are using monte carlo method?
> > > > > > Do we have to run following codes (for the sake of examples)?:
> > > > > > mont_indirect <- monteCarloMed('a*b', object = fit, plot=T, CI=95)
> > > > > > mont_indirect
> > > > > > mont_direct <- monteCarloMed('c', object = fit, plot=T, CI=95)
> > > > > > mont_direct
> > > > > > mont_total <- monteCarloMed('a*b+c', object = fit, plot=T, CI=95)
> > > > > > mont_total
> > > > > >
> > > > > > And then we compare significance of mont_indirect and mont_direct to draw conclusions regarding full, partial, or no mediation. If yes, then what is the role of calculating mont_total, i.e. total effect?
> > > > > > On Wednesday, March 31, 2021 at 2:27:35 AM UTC+5:30 Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
> > > > > > > > would the Monte Carlo perform as well as bootstrapping in a large sample (e.g. N>10.000) ?
> > > > > > >
> > > > > > > I'm sure, but it depends on the model size.  In simple mediation models, the methods (including delta method) practically converge in samples barely over 100.
