Hi Terrence,

Thank you for your feedback.

As in the hierarchal model, I get negative observed variable variance for Bipolar Disorder when I perform a CFA with the 3 lower-order factors with and without std.lv <http://std.lv> = T. This negative variance problem is resolved when loading the observed variables of f3 (Bipolar + Schizophrenia) directly onto the P-Factor.

Question 1: How do I know from the factor correlations that a higher-order factor really fits the "observed" pattern? I included my code with the factor correlations and covariance.

Question 2: Why would you suggest not using std.lv <http://std.lv> = T to estimate factor scores? When I look at the correlation between the factor scores (P, f1, and f2) that I extract from the models with std.lv <http://std.lv> = T and std.lv <http://std.lv> = F using the EBM method with missing data, I get a perfect correlation.

> twolevel.terrence.1 <- '
+   f1 =~ Anxiety.Disorder + Lifetime.Depression + PTSD + OCD + Self.Harm
+   f2 =~ Alcohol.Addiction + Other.Substance.Abuse
+   P=~ f1 + f2 + Bipolar.Disorder + Schizophrenia
+ '

> fit.std.lv.T <- cfa(twolevel.terrence.1, data= Problematic_DF, missing = "pairwise",
+                      ordered=TRUE, std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE)

> lavInspect(fit.std.lv.T, "cor.lv <http://cor.lv>")
   f1    f2    P
f1 1.000
f2 0.693 1.000
P  0.891 0.778 1.000

> lavInspect(fit.std.lv.T, "cov.lv <http://cov.lv>")
   f1    f2    P
f1 4.848
f2 2.426 2.529
P  1.962 1.236 1.000

> fitMeasures(fit.std.lv.T, c("chisq", "df", "pvalue", "cfi", "tli", "srmr", "rmsea"))

  chisq      df  pvalue     cfi     tli    srmr   rmsea

732.811  25.000   0.000   0.991   0.987   0.084   0.017

> fit.std.lv.F <- cfa(twolevel.terrence.1, data= Problematic_DF, missing = "pairwise",
+                      ordered=TRUE, std.lv <http://std.lv>=FALSE)

> lavInspect(ffit.std.lv.F, "cov.lv <http://cov.lv>")
   f1    f2    P
f1 0.579
f2 0.391 0.549
P  0.460 0.391 0.460

Thank you very much!

Camille

On Sunday, June 13, 2021 at 5:28:22 PM UTC+2 Terrence Jorgensen wrote:
> It is easier to simply set std.lv <http://std.lv>=TRUE than free first loadings and fix factor variances.  But in this case I think you should stick with the default method of fixing the first loading and freeing the factor variances.  It looks like the P=~f1 loading is trying its hardest to become orders of magnitude larger than its residual variance, which you have fixed to 1 (so it can't compensate by approaching zero).   In fact, before fitting a higher-order factor, just fit a CFA with the 3 lower-order factors.  With only 3 factors, that is statistically equivalent anyway, but you could take a look at the factor correlations and see whether a higher-order factor really fits the "observed" pattern.
